# .gitignore template for an Obsidian vault kept in git.
#
# Copy to the vault root as `.gitignore` and commit it FIRST — before the first
# `git add -A`. Git stores every version of every blob it has ever tracked, for
# ever: a plugin cache added once and ignored afterwards still lives in the pack
# files, and getting it out means rewriting history in both clones. The cheap
# moment is the one before the first commit.
#
# What follows is grouped by *why*, because the reasons are different and an
# operator adapting this needs to know which rules are taste and which are load
# bearing.


# ── 1. Editor state that is per-machine, not per-vault ───────────────────────
#
# `workspace.json` records which panes are open, their sizes, the active file
# and the recent-files list. Obsidian rewrites it constantly — on every pane
# change, so many times a minute while somebody is working — and its content is
# meaningless on the other machine.
#
# Tracked, it is the single worst rule in this file: it produces a conflicting
# commit almost every sweep, on both clones, and those conflicts are what make
# people give up on syncing a vault with git. Everything else in `.obsidian/`
# (settings, hotkeys, themes, plugin configuration) is genuinely shared and
# genuinely worth versioning, so the exclusion is these two files by name
# rather than the whole directory.
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json


# ── 2. The soft-delete bin ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `delete_note` and `delete_file` rename into `.trash/` under a timestamped
# name rather than unlinking, and Obsidian's own "move to trash" does the same.
# Tracking it would keep every deleted note's bytes in the repository for ever
# under a name nobody will ever look up — while the deletion commit's parent
# already holds those bytes, addressable by path and by date. A recycle bin is
# not history.
.trash/


# ── 3. Filesystem litter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini


# ── 4. Plugin caches and generated indexes — the size rule ───────────────────
#
# This is the section that decides whether the repository stays a few hundred
# megabytes or becomes tens of gigabytes.
#
# Several popular plugins keep a derived index *inside the vault*: embeddings,
# full-text indexes, link graphs, transcription caches. In a real vault these
# reach hundreds of megabytes, they are rewritten wholesale rather than
# incrementally, and they are pure functions of content that is tracked anyway —
# so every sweep commits a fresh copy of the whole thing, and `git gc` cannot
# help because consecutive versions of a binary or line-shuffled index do not
# delta well. A vault that would have been 300 MB of markdown becomes an
# unclonable repository within weeks.
#
# The patterns are written by *shape* rather than by plugin name, because the
# plugin list changes and an operator will not revisit this file. Two named
# examples are called out where the shape alone would not catch them.

# Any cache-shaped directory under a plugin, at any depth.
.obsidian/plugins/**/cache/
.obsidian/plugins/**/.cache/
.obsidian/plugins/**/caches/
.obsidian/plugins/**/tmp/
.obsidian/plugins/**/temp/

# Any index-shaped directory or file under a plugin. `.obsidian/plugins/*/data.json`
# is deliberately NOT here: that is a plugin's *settings*, which are small and
# worth versioning.
.obsidian/plugins/**/index/
.obsidian/plugins/**/indexes/
.obsidian/plugins/**/*.index
.obsidian/plugins/**/*.idx
.obsidian/plugins/**/*.db
.obsidian/plugins/**/*.sqlite
.obsidian/plugins/**/*.sqlite3

# Embedding and vector stores, wherever a plugin decided to put them.
**/*.ajson
**/embeddings/
**/*.embeddings.json
**/*.vectors.json

# Smart Connections (`.smart-env/`) and Copilot (`copilot-index/`) write outside
# `.obsidian/`, at the vault root, in shard files that a shape-based rule above
# would otherwise miss entirely. `*.ajson` above is Smart Connections' shard
# format; these cover the directories themselves.
.smart-env/
.smart-connections/
copilot-index/

# Obsidian's own generated caches.
.obsidian/cache
.obsidian/graph.json.bak

# Excalidraw and similar plugins download scripts and fonts into the vault.
# They are third-party code, restored by the plugin on demand, and large.
**/Excalidraw/Scripts/Downloaded/


# ── 5. A nested vault's own configuration ────────────────────────────────────
#
# A vault often contains another vault as a subdirectory — an archive opened
# separately, a shared folder somebody else's Obsidian also points at. Its
# `.obsidian/` is that vault's configuration, not this one's: it has its own
# `workspace.json` churning on its own machine, its own plugin caches (which the
# rules above are anchored at `.obsidian/…` and therefore do NOT reach at a
# nested path), and versioning it here means two vaults fighting over one file.
#
# The pattern excludes `.obsidian/` at every depth and then re-includes this
# vault's own, at the root. Order matters: the negation must come after the
# exclusion, and the leading `/` is what makes it mean "the root one, and only
# that one".
**/.obsidian/
!/.obsidian/
